// Logging over the build-selected provider.
//
// A `Logger` is an ordinary service handle: a `dyn mut` view of a sink, the same
// shape `fmt.Writer` has. Nothing about it is compiler-known. What the
// program selects is which one the package-level procedures below dispatch to,
// and `-log-level` decides which of them have a body at all.
//
// There is no scoped or package-local override of the program logger. A package
// that needs a different sink, captured test output, or request-specific fields
// takes a `Logger` parameter or keeps one in a state object.
package log;

// One logging sink. Any record with this `write` is one; the message carries no
// line ending.
Sink :: interface($Self: type) {
	slot write: proc(self: inout Self, level: Level, message: string_view);
}

// The handle a program logs through: a view of a sink that lives as long as the
// process, since a factory's result is kept for the life of the program.
Logger :: dyn mut Sink;

// The handle the program is logging through, for code that wants to pass it on
// rather than call the package procedures.
current :: proc() -> Logger ---;

// The standard logger: one line per record on the process error stream, with
// the level in front of it. It is the fallback *and* an ordinary provider — a
// build may select `log.standard_logger` explicitly to say so.
standard_logger :: proc() -> Logger ---;

debug :: proc(args: ..any_view) ---;

info :: proc(args: ..any_view) ---;

warning :: proc(args: ..any_view) ---;

error :: proc(args: ..any_view) ---;

// Is anything at this level compiled in? A caller with an expensive argument
// asks the constant directly in a `when`; this is for a runtime branch over a
// level held in a variable. `Off` answers false.
enabled :: proc(level: Level) -> bool ---;

// A record at a level chosen at runtime, which is what `enabled` above is for:
// having branched on a level held in a variable, this is how a caller records
// at it without switching over five members to reach one of the four fixed
// procedures. An `off` build discards it too.
at :: proc(level: Level, args: ..any_view) ---;
